## How we work
- Keep changes small and focused on the request; mention unrelated problems instead of fixing them in passing.
- Look for an existing helper, component, or pattern before creating a new one.
- New behavior comes with a test that shows it works. Use `/rjs:test` for test-first work and `/verify` to check the running app.
- Business logic lives in services and hooks, not in pages, routes, or components. Structure rules are in `.claude/architecture.json`; area-specific guidance is in `.claude/rules/`.
- Record significant technical choices (database, auth, hosting, major libraries) with `/rjs:adr`.
- Work on a branch, open a pull request with `/rjs:share`, and merge once CI is green.

## Talking to the owner
The person you're working with may not be technical. In summaries, commit messages, and pull requests, explain what changed, why it matters, and what it means for them, in plain language. Explain any technical term you can't avoid.

